WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
- I ranked "The Good Doctor"

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.860
as the second most accurate medical drama

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:04.693
of all time.

00:00:04.693 --> 00:00:07.510
And then ABC gave me
an exclusive first look

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:10.040
at the Season 5 premiere,
which premieres Monday,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.400
September 27th, 10/9 Central on ABC.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.100
Let's see what Dr. Shaun
Murphy's up to the season,

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.981
and huge thank you to ABC
for sponsoring this video.

00:00:18.981 --> 00:00:19.850
Pew!

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.030
- Shaun Robert.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.430
- Aw, look at them getting married.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:23.890
Everyone's suited up.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.257
Oh, storm incoming.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:26.090
(thunder rumbles)

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.207
(classical music)

00:00:27.207 --> 00:00:28.530
(thunder claps)

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:29.790
Remember those who are diagnosed

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.160
with autism spectrum
disorder are susceptible

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.630
to really loud sounds.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:34.970
They hear them louder.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.750
The processing of auditory
stimuli is different.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.070
It can be very uncomfortable,

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.310
so much so that they
can have a panic attack

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.238
just because of a loud sound
or other external stimuli.

00:00:44.238 --> 00:00:47.160
(classical music)

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.610
Definitely the first
gentlemen to wear earplugs

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.253
in the middle of his wedding.

00:00:52.253 --> 00:00:54.824
(thunder claps)

00:00:54.824 --> 00:00:56.530
(guests screaming)

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.750
What's wedding, like, norms?

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.510
Is there bad luck if it's thundering

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:02.820
and pouring during your wedding?

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.980
Or is that like good luck,
like a pigeon pooping on you?

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.531
(thunder claps)

00:01:06.531 --> 00:01:07.700
(screams intensify)

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.533
Oh no!

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.740
- Hi.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:11.980
- I had a great time last night.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.470
- Me too. Wanna go grab some breakfast?

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.050
- I gotta get to the hospital.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.557
- Oh, don't do that doctor thing,

00:01:17.557 --> 00:01:19.110
"I gotta get to the hospital."

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.060
If you're having a person spend the night,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.950
eat breakfast, enjoy each
other's company, plan for it.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.210
- Perhaps, I should let you go.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.043
- No, no.

00:01:26.043 --> 00:01:29.800
I was hoping for a little
excitement this morning.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.382
- Mm, spicy.

00:01:31.382 --> 00:01:34.000
(knocking on door)

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.700
- Just give me two minutes
to make this go away. Yeah?

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.700
- Ooh. So much romance, and
the episode just started.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.300
- Some shortness of breath,
occasional blurred vision

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.770
and headaches, and this
morning, my urine was foamy.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:49.630
- Is she pregnant?

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:50.710
'Cause that could be a problem.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.310
- Any blood in your urine?

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.143
- A little.

00:01:53.143 --> 00:01:54.810
- You take 20 milligrams
of Ritalin LA a day?

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:55.950
- For my ADHD.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.430
- Blurred vision and headaches

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.000
can be side effects from Ritalin.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.520
Would you be willing
to stop your medication

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.360
for a couple of days?

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.130
See if they're causing
any of your symptoms?

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.350
- I'll refer any complaints
about my behavior to you.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.100
- What's interesting to me
is she's having a wide range

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.040
of symptoms outside of
blurry vision and headaches,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.060
that warrants further investigation.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.650
Whenever you're trying to decide

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.050
whether or not it's due to
a medication side effect,

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.420
you're trying to rule
out any organic causes,

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.870
then you could try doing
things with medications,

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.020
but jumping to medications
right off the bat,

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.820
unless it's like a really
well-known side effect

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.760
and it's only that one symptom,
doesn't usually happen.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.760
- We're gonna run some labs
into a CT of your kidneys.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.230
- Let's say the foamy urine

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.320
could be a sign of kidney stones.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.180
The first test you would do
there wouldn't be a CT scan,

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.500
unless the patient was having
other symptoms like back pain.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.470
So I would do like a urine dipstick test,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.420
make sure that we have the
presence of blood there,

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.360
then send it out for a urine microscopy,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.570
see if there's crystals there.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:47.910
And then what you can do,

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.460
is you do a 24 hour urine collection

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.260
to see the total amount
of the calcium oxalate

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.573
or uric acid or whatnot in the urine.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.310
- Renal pelvis looks clean.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.250
- You sound disappointed.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:02.083
- But what's that?

00:03:02.083 --> 00:03:03.070
On the right kidney.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.000
Could be a mass.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.350
She needs a kidney biopsy.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:08.610
- Interesting for conditions like this,

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.850
we usually page the
interventional radiologist,

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.420
as well as the oncologist to decide

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.830
if we see suspicious features on the scan.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.150
There's also conditions like
Polycystic Kidney Disease,

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.260
which just develop a lot
of cysts in your kidneys,

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.570
potentially leading to
needing a kidney transplant

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.773
or even having it become a fatal disease.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.560
- Have you always had fat ankles?

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:30.393
- Rude.

00:03:30.393 --> 00:03:31.280
- Only if the answer is yes,

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.760
if the answer is no, it's a symptom.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.910
- Edema in the lower extremities

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:37.640
for a young person could
be a sign of proteinuria,

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.890
which means she's going
into kidney failure.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.510
She could have a nephrotic syndrome.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:45.280
She has blood in her urine
so she could have like FSG

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.780
caused by HIV.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.730
There's a lot of possibilities here.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.360
- Are you overwhelmed
planning the wedding?

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.727
Because Park and Lim thought
you might be overwhelmed.

00:03:53.727 --> 00:03:55.015
- Nope, not at all.

00:03:55.015 --> 00:03:56.720
I've got it. It's gonna be great.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:57.553
- Great.

00:03:57.553 --> 00:03:59.980
- There should be a term called whelm.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.410
What if there's not too
much, but there's a lot.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:03.243
Whelmed.

00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:05.170
- Nice exposure of the right tonsil.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:06.680
Visibility can be tough with kids.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:09.510
- That's true. When you're
doing any kind of procedure

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.160
in their oral pharyngeal space,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.790
it's sometimes hard to get a visual.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.690
- There's a firm, irregular
mass in this tonsil.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:17.730
- Tonsil stone?

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:18.563
- Get that to pathology.

00:04:18.563 --> 00:04:19.990
- I didn't quite see it.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:21.310
It looked like a tonsil stone.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.570
I hope it's not a tumor of any sort.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.210
- Riley's throat, it's
still really bothering him.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:27.940
- We had to remove additional tissue

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.890
because your son's tonsils
showed signs of cancerous cells.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:32.900
- Riley has cancer?

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.840
- The cancer on your son's
tonsil is cervical cancer.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:36.673
- Interesting.

00:04:36.673 --> 00:04:39.830
Cervical cancer in the
sense of HPV induced cancer.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.960
- He got it from you.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.520
He was exposed to your cancerous
cells in the birth canal,

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.950
which he likely inhaled.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.870
Jackson may also have
been exposed during birth.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.600
We need to scan both you-

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.990
- We definitely need to find
out her pap smear results

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.120
because that is how we
screen for cervical cancer.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.480
The human papilloma virus HPV
has certain high-risk subtypes

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.310
that predispose you to
the development of cancer.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.200
That's why we recommend
patients to get vaccinated

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.990
against HPV in order to prevent the virus

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.360
thereby preventing the cancer.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.400
It's actually one of the
only vaccines that we have

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.170
that prevents cancer.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.960
- The PET scan shows
increased metabolic activity

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.680
in the mother's uterus
and the son's trachea.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:18.513
- Oh no.

00:05:18.513 --> 00:05:19.523
- They both have cancer.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:22.590
- Yeah. That's the terrible part

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:26.010
about human papilloma virus,
they can cause cancer there

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.240
in the anal genital region,

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.780
oral cavity in the oral mucosa
as seen with this child.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.280
And then it could spread,
if it is advanced.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:37.430
- Your cancer is quite advanced.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.470
Most cervical cancers are detected early

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.350
with regular pap smears.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:42.550
- This is a really important point.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.510
Not only should you be
getting regular visits

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.760
with your doctor for a
whole host of reasons,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.930
but you should be making
sure that your pap smears

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:52.260
are up to date from the age of 21.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.930
It means that from the age of 21,

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.250
you start getting them every three years.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.980
Once you hit the age of 30,
the guidelines now change

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:03.760
and say that we should do pap
smears, plus HPV cotesting,

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.450
which means we also check
for HPV at the same time.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.670
And if both are negative at that time,

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.999
you can spread out to every five years.

00:06:10.999 --> 00:06:14.416
(intense dramatic music)

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.863
- Endoscopic mucosal tracheal resection.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.720
If Jackson's tumor was seeded
via exposure during birth,

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.360
which is a very reasonable assumption,

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.250
then we can reasonably
assume that it is localized.

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.810
Endoscopic resection
should allow for a recovery

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.233
of days instead of weeks.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.010
- Talk to the mother.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:35.410
- Talk to the surgical team.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:36.990
Why didn't they figure that out?

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.277
Shaun is a super surgeon.

00:06:39.277 --> 00:06:41.600
(machines beeping)

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:43.440
- [Surgeon] You're at the mass.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.333
- [Shaun] It has a wide base.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.380
No easy way to loop my snare around it.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:49.400
- Oh no. Is it?

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.300
- [Surgeon] Is it pulsating?
- Oh no.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:52.470
- Is it a blood vessel?

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:55.170
Is it around, is it
surrounding a blood vessel?

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:58.060
- [Surgeon] It has a major
arterial feeder from his aorta.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.130
We can't remove it from this angle

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:01.680
without risking a massive bleed.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.330
- The interesting part
about tumors is that they go

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:06.450
through a process called angiogenesis,

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.630
which is the formation
of new blood vessels.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:10.360
They do this in order
to keep themselves alive

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:11.920
and to feed themselves to grow.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:14.340
And in those cases, you have
to perform an open surgery

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.420
where you actually go in

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.340
and separate the blood vessel carefully,

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.110
while making sure that you're
having adequate blood supply

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:21.650
throughout the rest of the body.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:22.650
- Abort the surgery.

00:07:23.559 --> 00:07:25.790
(intense dramatic music)

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:27.140
- I think I know what they're gonna do.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:28.260
And I'm not a surgeon.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:29.430
I'm gonna try and predict it.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.730
I think that they're gonna do
a radical coil embolization

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.630
where they're gonna go
in through either a vein

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.890
or the femoral vein, put in a catheter

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:41.753
and they're gonna embolize it
like as if it's an aneurysm.

00:07:41.753 --> 00:07:44.420
(playful music)

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:46.883
Dude, are you serious?

00:07:46.883 --> 00:07:48.620
- [Man] Hey, hey.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:51.223
- I would like to make a toast.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.900
I have never ever seen you as happy

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:59.120
as you are right now with Lea.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.480
- He does look happier.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.220
Like, when I think about the first episode

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:03.860
of "The Good Doctor" that
I watched on this channel,

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.573
he looks much happier here.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.080
- There's been some ups and downs,

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.710
little bit like a roller coaster.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:10.543
- Okay.

00:08:10.543 --> 00:08:13.853
- You guys have managed it so well.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.837
A whole lot better than I have.

00:08:17.837 --> 00:08:20.970
(guests laughing)

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:21.803
Is that funny?

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.730
But here's to many, many, many

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:32.340
years of

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:33.173
happiness.

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:39.074
At least two, maybe three.

00:08:39.074 --> 00:08:41.824
(dramatic music)

00:08:46.777 --> 00:08:49.280
- What?! That is not where
you make a relevation,

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.220
revelation of that caliber!

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:53.520
What are you doing?

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.300
That was viscerally painful to watch.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:58.263
- Dr. Lim.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:00.550
- Excuse me, who are you?

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:02.390
And what are you doing
in my operating room?

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:03.940
- I'm Salen Morrison.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.770
- What are you doing there?

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:08.360
You're not surgically
prepared to be there.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.680
That's dangerous for all parties involved.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.060
- Please page Dr. Andrews and
have him come get his patient.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.670
- Or security.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.190
(machines beeping)

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:18.040
- Oh, I feel...

00:09:19.341 --> 00:09:20.920
(machines beeping)

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:22.320
Something's wrong.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.153
- Stat.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.990
- What causes kidney
lesions, shortness of breath,

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:29.061
swollen ankles and heart irregularities?

00:09:29.061 --> 00:09:30.360
- Ahhh.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.400
Amyloid deposits.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.220
A tumor that secretes

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.640
some sort of hormone.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:38.810
- Every time we get close to
doing the biopsy on this woman,

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:41.110
something dark blocks us.

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:42.223
- Unless.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:45.610
Look at her blood work.

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:46.870
She said she'd stop her meds,

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:50.000
but her Ritalin level today is
over double when she came in.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.020
That could cause her heart irregularities.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:52.853
- That could.

00:09:52.853 --> 00:09:54.042
- She is...

00:09:54.042 --> 00:09:56.792
(suspense music)

00:09:58.966 --> 00:10:01.350
- What?! This is like out of this world.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.210
It doesn't happen, but it could happen.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:06.180
Click here to check out a full playlist

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.090
of all my "Good Doctor" reactions.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.170
And don't forget to check
out the season premiere

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:14.720
of "The Good Doctor", September
27th, 10/9 Central on ABC.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.760
Huge thanks to ABC for
sponsoring this video

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.358
and as always stay happy and healthy.

00:10:19.358 --> 00:10:21.734
(upbeat music)

